hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1500","Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi informed us, from Asim, from Abu Uthman, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b; and Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la al-San'ani narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, from his father, Abu Uthman narrated to us, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b; and Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b - and this is the hadith of Abbad - he said:

There was a man from the Ansar whose house was the farthest house in Madinah, yet he never missed praying (Salah) with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I felt pity for him, so I said, ""O so-and-so, if only you bought a donkey that would protect you from the hot ground, lift you up from the mud, and protect you from the crawling creatures of the earth."" He said, ""By Allah, I would not love for my house to be joined by a tent-rope to the house of Muhammad ﷺ.""

He (the narrator) said: I carried this matter with me until I came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him. He said: So he called him and asked him, and he mentioned to him the same thing, and mentioned that he hoped for reward in his"
"1501","Muhammad bin Al-Ala bin Kurayb and Musa bin Abdul-Rahman Al-Masruqi told us, they said: Abu Usama told us, on the authority of Burayd, on the authority of Abu Burdah, On the authority of Abu Musa, he said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: “Indeed, the people with the greatest reward for prayer are the ones who walk the furthest to it, so they are farthest away.” “The one who waits for the prayer until he prays it with the imam in congregation will have a greater reward than the one who prays it and then sleeps.” They are all one word."
